; A purchase at a fixated cost, `10 AAPL @ =$5`, creates the lot
; {=$5} [2020/01/01], and a later posting that names that lot,
; `10 AAPL {=$5} [2020/01/01]`, adds to it: the running total reaches
; 20 AAPL {=$5} [2020/01/01] at the second Assets:Broker posting, and
; Assets and the grand total, net of the -10 on Assets:Cash, hold 10 of
; that one lot.
;
; `@ =` gives the purchase the fixated lot itself, the lot that a posting
; writing {=$5} [2020/01/01] names, so the two postings reach one lot.
; Were it instead to mark the lot its cost creates, {$5} [2020/01/01], as
; fixated, that lot would print as {=$5} [2020/01/01] but still be found
; only as {$5} [2020/01/01]: the later posting would open a second lot
; with the same annotation, which a balance would keep apart from the
; first, so the running total would show 10 AAPL twice, and the grand
; total 20 and -10 AAPL.

2020/01/01 Buy
    Assets:Broker    10 AAPL @ =$5
    Assets:Cash

2020/01/02 Transfer in
    Assets:Broker    10 AAPL {=$5} [2020/01/01]
    Assets:Cash

test reg --lots
20-Jan-01 Buy                   Assets:Broker          10 AAPL {=$5} [2020/01/01] 10 AAPL {=$5} [2020/01/01]
                                Assets:Cash                    $-50         $-50
                                                      10 AAPL {=$5} [2020/01/01]
20-Jan-02 Transfer in           Assets:Broker          10 AAPL {=$5} [2020/01/01]         $-50
                                                      20 AAPL {=$5} [2020/01/01]
                                Assets:Cash            -10 AAPL {=$5} [2020/01/01]         $-50
                                                      10 AAPL {=$5} [2020/01/01]
end test

test bal --lots
                      $-50
10 AAPL {=$5} [2020/01/01]  Assets
20 AAPL {=$5} [2020/01/01]    Broker
                       $-50
-10 AAPL {=$5} [2020/01/01]    Cash
--------------------
                      $-50
10 AAPL {=$5} [2020/01/01]
end test
